  "worked_solution": "Let $T$ be the event that a chosen student obtained the top grade, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the student is from Group A,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the student is from Group B,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the student is from Group С.\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student obtained the top grade, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(T\\cap A)+P(T\\cap B)+P(T\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|B)P(B)+P(T|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $35%$ of students are in Group A, $35%$ are in Group B, and $30%$ are in Group C, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.35,\\,P(B)=0.35,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of the students in Group A, and $10%$ in Group B obtained the top grade, we have\n\n$$\nP(T|A)=0.2,\\,P(T|B)=0.1\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected student from any group obtained the top grade is $0.255,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=0.255.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(T|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|B)P(B)+P(T|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.255 & =(0.2)(0.35)+(0.1)(0.35)+P(T|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.255 & =0.07+0.035+0.3P(T|C) \\\\ 0.3P(T|C) & =0.15 \\\\ P(T|C) & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student from Group C obtained the top grade is $0.50.$",
